.TH EVENT_LOOP_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_create_with_mprocs \- create a persistent epoll-based event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

event_loop event_loop_create_with_mprocs(size_t max_events_per_wait,
                        size_t num_lock_stripes, size_t num_reactor_threads,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a persistent epoll(7) instance and immediately spawns the
thread(s) that drive it.  Unlike ccol_select(), which creates a fresh
epoll instance on every call and tears it down after exactly one ready
selectable, an event_loop's epoll instance and thread(s) live for as
long as the handle does, and registrations (event_loop_add /
event_loop_modify / event_loop_remove) are mutated incrementally
against it.

Exactly ONE dedicated thread ever calls epoll_wait, for every
num_reactor_threads value; never more than one, regardless of how
large num_reactor_threads is.  num_reactor_threads == 1 reproduces this
module's original single-thread design exactly, byte-for-byte: that one
thread both polls and runs every callback inline.  A larger value spawns
that same one polling thread plus (num_reactor_threads - 1) separate
dispatch worker threads that actually execute callbacks, so total OS
thread count for a given num_reactor_threads is always exactly that
value in both configurations.  Every thread is ready to work as soon as
this call returns, mirroring create_cthread_pool()'s "ready to work the
moment you get the handle" ergonomics.

With num_reactor_threads > 1, two correctness properties hold that a
single thread gets for free from having only one caller: a single
registration's callback(s) are never invoked concurrently with
themselves, and a read registration and a write registration sharing
the same fd never run concurrently with each other either (stricter
than "no self-concurrency" alone, so a callback pair that shares state
across both directions of one fd, such as one TLS connection object,
needs no locking of its own).  A second dispatch for the same
registration is also never collected while an earlier one is still
queued or executing, so application code calling event_loop_modify from
within an in-flight callback (a supported, commonly used pattern)
never races a concurrently-collected second dispatch for that same
registration.  See event_loop_reg_generation(3) for the separate,
additional caller-visible identity token this design also makes
available.

Registrations are built from the same ccol_selectable type ccol_select
uses (selectable_from_fd, selectable_from_circq, selectable_from_dynq,
selectable_from_chan).  Like ccol_select, event_loop never performs the
receive or send itself, for any selectable type; the callback always
does so explicitly.  See event_loop_add(3) for the full rationale.

Internally, one persistent epoll interest-list entry backs each
registered fd (both directions share one entry, since epoll_ctl keys its
interest list by fd, not by (fd, direction) pair), while each queue or
channel selectable gets its own dedicated bridge eventfd(2) permanently
linked into the queue's own waiter list; not the transient, per-call
waiter nodes ccol_select() uses.

The fd/registration registry is lock-striped: num_lock_stripes
independent (mutex, chmap) pairs, each guarding a disjoint subset of
registrations.  A real fd, or a queue/channel registration's private
bridge eventfd, is always handled by exactly one stripe for its entire
lifetime, so no operation ever needs more than one stripe's lock.
Passing 1 reproduces the original single-lock design exactly (one mutex,
one chmap), just with one extra array indirection.  Passing a larger
value lets event_loop_add/event_loop_remove/event_loop_modify calls for
different fds/registrations proceed concurrently instead of serializing
through a single lock, at the cost of num_lock_stripes mutexes and
chmaps allocated up front.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_events_per_wait  Size of the epoll_wait batch buffer the polling
                      thread uses (must be >= 1).
num_lock_stripes     Number of independent lock stripes for the fd/entry
                      registry (must be >= 1).  1 matches the original
                      single-lock behavior.  No upper bound is enforced.
num_reactor_threads  Total OS thread count devoted to this loop's own
                      polling and dispatch (must be >= 1).  1 matches
                      this module's original single-thread behavior
                      exactly (one thread polls and dispatches inline);
                      any larger value means exactly one dedicated
                      polling thread plus (num_reactor_threads - 1)
                      dispatch worker threads.
mmgmt_procs          Custom memory management procedures, or NULL for
                      default malloc/free.
err_str               Optional pointer to receive an error string on
                      failure (pass NULL to ignore).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new event_loop handle on success.
Returns NULL on failure: max_events_per_wait is 0, num_lock_stripes is 0,
num_reactor_threads is 0, mmgmt_procs is malformed (some but not all of
malloc/calloc/realloc/free set), allocation failure, or
epoll_create1(2)/eventfd(2)/pthread_create(3) failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Multiple independent event_loop instances may coexist in the same
process; each owns its own epoll instance, reactor thread(s), and lock
stripe array, and shares no global state with any other instance
(including num_lock_stripes and num_reactor_threads, both genuinely
per-instance settings).

This module originally let every one of num_reactor_threads threads call
epoll_wait independently on the shared epoll instance. That design was
replaced with the single-poller-plus-worker-pool design described above
after being measured, not assumed, to cost real single-connection tail
latency: with N threads each independently blocked in epoll_wait on one
shared epoll instance, a single ready event genuinely wakes every one of
them (a real kernel-level thundering herd; EPOLLEXCLUSIVE does not help,
since it governs the same fd registered across multiple SEPARATE epoll
instances, not many threads sharing one), and that wasted wakeup cost
measurably hurt p95/p99 latency for a single, otherwise-uncontended
connection the moment more than one thread was configured at all. The
current design eliminates that cost structurally (only one thread ever
blocks in epoll_wait, so there is nothing left to wake but that one
thread) while still providing multi-threaded dispatch throughput under
real concurrent load via the separate worker pool. num_reactor_threads
== 1 measures byte-for-byte identical to the original single-thread
design, confirming this change was purely additive for that
configuration.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call concurrently with unrelated event_loop instances.  Not
meaningful to call concurrently "on" a handle that does not exist yet.
.fi
